## 4. Selection and sizing

- The thermal class of the equipment, since PET is chosen only where the class allows it.
- Whether the tube is assembled by hand or by machine, which sets the length tolerance and the packaging format.
- Wall and bore, against the lead or winding it covers.
- Whether the part has to resist a solvent or a varnish used in the winding process.
- The number of small batch sizes involved, which is usually the real cost driver.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 60674-3-2, requirements for biaxially oriented polyethylene terephthalate films for electrical purposes.
- IEC 60085, thermal evaluation and classification of electrical insulation, for the class declared.
- UL 94, flammability where declared.
- ASTM D882, tensile properties of thin plastic sheeting.
- IEC 60684 series, flexible insulating sleeving, for sleeving requirements generally.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Avoid sharp edges on the lead or core before the tube is fitted, since thin film splits at a burr.
- Do not force the tube over a lead that is at the bore limit.
- Keep the parts dry and clean before winding.
- Store in a size-labelled format, since thin wall gauges are visually similar.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tube splits during assembly | Burr on the lead, or a bore at the limit of the range | Deburr the lead and select the bore with clearance. |
| Insulation resistance low after varnish cure | Film not compatible with the process temperature or the solvent used | Confirm the film against the full process exposure, not only the service temperature. |
| Length tolerance rejects the batch | Cut length specified without the assembly method in mind | Agree the cut tolerance against the assembly method. |
| Material substituted without approval | Cost pressure leading to a different film grade | Confirm that any substitution is assessed against the thermal class and the process exposure. |

## 8. Typical applications

- Motor and compressor winding insulation and lead covering.
- Transformer lead insulation and coil winding.
- Relay, solenoid and small electrical assembly insulation.
- General low temperature electrical insulation where cost is the deciding factor.

